        </details>

        <details class="notice" id="smartscreen-details">
          <summary>Why can the EXE installer trigger a SmartScreen warning?</summary>
          <div class="notice__contenu prose">
            <p>
              SmartScreen can appear on the classic EXE installer, especially for recent or
              rarely downloaded files. AZERTY Global is open source and can be checked on
              <a href="https://github.com/AMCF-asso">GitHub</a>,
              but SmartScreen reputation also depends on the download channel and the number
              of installations.
            </p>
            <p>
              The EXE installer copies the layout into <code>C:\Windows\System32</code>, where
              Windows stores its keyboard layouts: that is why it needs admin rights.
            </p>
            <p>
              The Microsoft Store app touches no system files, asks for no admin rights and triggers
              no SmartScreen warning. If the Store is blocked, use the signed MSIX above.
            </p>
            <p><strong>To get past the warning:</strong> click
            “More info”, then “Run anyway”.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
