  <section class="section">
    <div class="conteneur">
      <h2>What changes in practice</h2>
      <div class="prose texte-2">
        <p>The letters A to Z, the accented letters (except ù) and the vast majority of symbols
        stay where they are. Here are the five changes.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="changements">
        <details class="changement" open>
          <summary><span class="changement__caracteres" aria-hidden="true">É Ç</span>Smart Caps Lock</summary>
          <div class="changement__contenu prose">
            <p>With <kbd>Caps&nbsp;Lock</kbd> on, accented letters become capitals:
            <span class="insecable"><kbd>é</kbd> → É,</span> <span class="insecable"><kbd>è</kbd> → È,</span>
            <span class="insecable"><kbd>à</kbd> → À,</span> <span class="insecable"><kbd>ç</kbd> → Ç.</span> Punctuation and
            numbers are not affected. <a href="/e-aigu-majuscule" hreflang="fr">More about the capital É<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="changement">
          <summary><span class="changement__caracteres changement__caracteres--grand changement__caracteres--point" aria-hidden="true">.</span>The period without Shift</summary>
          <div class="changement__contenu prose">
            <p>The period types without <kbd>Shift</kbd>. The semicolon moves
            to <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>;</kbd>.</span></p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="changement">
          <summary><span class="changement__caracteres" aria-hidden="true">@ <span class="caractere-renforce">#</span></span>At sign and hash at the top left</summary>
          <div class="changement__contenu prose">
            <p>The at sign and the hash take over the former <kbd>²</kbd> key, to the left of the
            <kbd>1</kbd>, as on the macOS AZERTY. The hash is also available with
            <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>:</kbd>.</span> <a href="/arobase" hreflang="fr">The at sign page<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="changement">
          <summary><span class="changement__caracteres" aria-hidden="true">{ }</span>Programming symbols on the home row</summary>
          <div class="changement__contenu prose">
            <p>On the home row with <kbd>AltGr</kbd>: <kbd>{</kbd> <kbd>}</kbd> under the left middle
            and index fingers (D, F), <kbd>\</kbd> <kbd>|</kbd> in the center (G, H),
            <kbd>[</kbd> <kbd>]</kbd> under the right index and middle fingers (J, K). Plus comfortable access
            to <kbd>~</kbd>, <kbd>`</kbd>, <kbd>^</kbd> and <kbd>#</kbd>.
            <a href="/dev" hreflang="fr">The developers page<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details class="changement">
          <summary><span class="changement__caracteres changement__caracteres--grand changement__caracteres--accents" aria-hidden="true">´ ` ~</span>International accents on the ù key</summary>
          <div class="changement__contenu prose">
            <p>The <kbd>ù</kbd> and <kbd>%</kbd> move to make room for the acute accent
            (á, é, í, ó, ú: Spanish, Hungarian, etc.), the grave accent (à, è, ì, ò, ù: Italian,
            French, etc.) and the tilde (ã, ñ, õ: Portuguese, Spanish, etc.). The ù stays available with
            <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>U</kbd>,</span> and the % moves to <span class="insecable"><kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>)</kbd>.</span></p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="conteneur">
      <h2>The characters you can type with AZERTY&nbsp;Global</h2>
      <div class="prose texte-2">
        <p>Beyond the five changes, whole families of characters become accessible,
        and the letters A to Z stay where they are.</p>
      </div>
      <ul class="familles">
        <li>
          <strong>French characters</strong>
          <span class="familles__exemples"><a href="/e-aigu-majuscule" hreflang="fr" aria-label="Capital É (page in French)">É</a> <a href="/e-grave-majuscule" hreflang="fr" aria-label="Capital È (page in French)">È</a> <a href="/a-grave-majuscule" hreflang="fr" aria-label="Capital À (page in French)">À</a> Ù <a href="/c-cedille-majuscule" hreflang="fr" aria-label="Capital Ç (page in French)">Ç</a> <a href="/e-dans-l-o" hreflang="fr" aria-label="Œ, e in the o (page in French)">Œ</a> <a href="/e-dans-l-a" hreflang="fr" aria-label="Æ, e in the a (page in French)">Æ</a> …</span>
          <span>Accented capitals come with <kbd>Caps&nbsp;Lock</kbd>;
          œ and æ without a dead key.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Programming symbols</strong>
          <span class="familles__exemples"><a href="/arobase" hreflang="fr" aria-label="At sign @ (page in French)">@</a> # <a href="/accolades" hreflang="fr" aria-label="Curly braces { } (page in French)">{&nbsp;}</a> <a href="/crochets" hreflang="fr" aria-label="Square brackets [ ] (page in French)">[&nbsp;]</a> \ …</span>
          <span>@ and # at the top left; the rest on the home row,
          with <kbd>AltGr</kbd>.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>French and international punctuation</strong>
          <span class="familles__exemples">. <a href="/guillemets" hreflang="fr" aria-label="French quotation marks « » (page in French)">«&nbsp;»</a> “ ” <a href="/tiret-cadratin" hreflang="fr" aria-label="Em dash — (page in French)">—</a> ¿ …</span>
          <span>Quotation marks, dashes and Spanish punctuation with <kbd>AltGr</kbd>; the period
          is direct, without <kbd>Shift</kbd>.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>European and African languages</strong>
          <span class="familles__exemples">ñ ø č ß å ŋ ɔ …</span>
          <span>Spanish, Danish, Polish, Czech, German, Swedish, Romanian
          and African languages: more than 300&nbsp;languages in total, counting Greek
          and Cyrillic.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Greek alphabet</strong>
          <span class="familles__exemples">α β γ δ π σ Ω …</span>
          <span>For math and physics, from high school to research. Statistics,
          engineering and finance use it too.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cyrillic alphabet</strong>
          <span class="familles__exemples">а б в г д е ж …</span>
          <span>Russian, Ukrainian, Belarusian, Bulgarian, Serbian and Macedonian; a second dead key adds Kazakh, Mongolian, Tatar, Bashkir and Tajik.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scientific, currency and miscellaneous symbols</strong>
          <span class="familles__exemples familles__exemples--symboles">∑ ∫ ≠ ∞ ₿ ★ ✈ …</span>
          <span>Through dead keys: <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>=</kbd></span>
          for science, <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>$</kbd></span>
          for currencies, <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>@</kbd></span> for
          miscellaneous symbols.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>International Phonetic Alphabet</strong>
          <span class="familles__exemples">ɑ ʃ ʒ ʁ ɥ ɪ ʏ …</span>
          <span>Through the dead key <span class="insecable"><kbd>AltGr</kbd> + <kbd>Shift</kbd> + <kbd>à</kbd></span>,
          to write down pronunciation.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="texte-petit texte-2">
        Details by use: <a href="/dev" hreflang="fr">developers<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>,
        <a href="/en/french-typography">French typography</a>,
        <a href="/afrique" hreflang="fr">African languages<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>,
        <a href="/francais-etranger" hreflang="fr">French outside France<span class="visuellement-cache"> (in French)</span></a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

